// repros/dyn_growth_with_window.flx — a dyn grown while a window is open
//
//   ./fluxa run repros/dyn_growth_with_window.flx      # run it eight times
//
// Growing a `dyn` past its end is documented and works. Doing it while a
// graphics window exists crashes the process about half the time, with no
// error — the shell reports a core dump.
//
// Measured on fluxa with raylib/6.0, miniaudio/0.11.25, in eight runs each:
//
//   growth alone, no libs at all ................ 8/8 ok
//   growth with std.image and image.new() ....... 8/8 ok
//   window open, no growth ...................... 8/8 ok
//   window open, grown to  20 elements .......... 8/8 ok
//   window open, grown to  40 elements .......... 7/8 ok
//   window open, grown to  80 elements .......... 5/8 ok
//   window open, grown to 160 elements .......... 3/8 ok
//
// So it is the two together, and the odds get worse with the number of times
// the list has to grow — which reads like a reallocation freeing something the
// collector still reaches, and the window making the collector run more often
// or at different moments. Nothing here touches the window after it opens.
//
// This is the shape of the code it was found in: an artwork building a list of
// points to hand to one call, with the stage already up.

import std graph

dyn win = graph.init(400, 300, "repro")

dyn p = [0.0]
int k = 1
while k < 160 {
    p[k] = 1.0
    k = k + 1
}

print("survived, len = ", len(p))
graph.close(win)
